    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0190" n="122" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg122"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72111/2313½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor for the Department of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Lansing</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to Dr. Charles Noble
                            Gregory
                    </hi><anchor n="27" xml:id="d118-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 7, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Gregory</hi>: I found time at last to read
                    carefully your paper on “Neutrality and Arms Shipments”<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d118-f2"/> and I wish now to congratulate you most heartily on the
                    comprehensive treatment of the subject and the conclusive and convincing
                    character of your arguments. I think you have left nothing unsaid and have
                    deprived those who are clamoring for an embargo without a leg to stand on.</p>
                <p>My regret is that the paper has not been given greater publicity. It deserves to
                    be read throughout the country.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d118-f1" n="27" type="source" xml:id="d118-f1-content">
                <p>Chairman of the Standing Committee on International Law of the American Bar
                    Association.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d118-f2" n="28" xml:id="d118-f2-content">
                <p>Published in the <hi rendition="#italic">New York Herald</hi>, May 16, 1915. The
                    file copy bears the notation in Mr. Lansing’s hand: “June 6, 1915. I have read
                    this with much interest and consider the arguments sound and convincing. It
                    should be used in case the question is officially discussed. Robert
                    Lansing.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
